WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.667
Web applications move data through several
interpreters.

2
00:00:03.747 --> 00:00:07.040
An HTTP value may enter a server template,
become HTML source,

3
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.553
produce DOM nodes, flow into a URL
property, or reach JavaScript or CSS processing.

4
00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:16.193
Cross-site scripting occurs when untrusted
data crosses one of those boundaries in a

5
00:00:16.273 --> 00:00:20.389
representation the browser can interpret
as active content instead of inert

6
00:00:20.469 --> 00:00:21.483
application data.

7
00:00:21.563 --> 00:00:25.679
The correct defense therefore depends on
what will interpret the value next.

8
00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:28.419
Reflected XSS returns request data in a
response,

9
00:00:28.499 --> 00:00:31.096
stored XSS persists data that is rendered
later,

10
00:00:31.176 --> 00:00:34.976
and DOM-based XSS is created by
client-side data flow and browser APIs.

11
00:00:35.056 --> 00:00:37.462
These labels describe where to
investigate,

12
00:00:37.542 --> 00:00:40.012
but they do not change the engineering
method.

13
00:00:40.092 --> 00:00:44.462
A value can be safely emitted by an API
and become unsafe when frontend code inserts

14
00:00:44.542 --> 00:00:46.125
it into an HTML-parsing sink.

15
00:00:46.205 --> 00:00:51.018
Server-rendered and client-rendered paths
both need source-to-destination analysis.

16
00:00:51.098 --> 00:00:55.912
The assignment container.innerHTML equals
userMessage is deliberately unsafe because

17
00:00:55.992 --> 00:00:59.348
it asks an HTML parser to create structure
from untrusted text.

18
00:00:59.428 --> 00:01:03.798
Assigning the same value to textContent
states the intended semantics directly:

19
00:01:03.878 --> 00:01:06.348
the value becomes a text node, not HTML
source.

20
00:01:06.428 --> 00:01:09.911
That is stronger than trying to identify
suspicious substrings.

21
00:01:09.991 --> 00:01:14.298
The browser never receives an instruction
to reinterpret the message as markup.

22
00:01:14.378 --> 00:01:17.228
TypeScript types do not alter this runtime
behavior;

23
00:01:17.308 --> 00:01:19.904
a string assertion cannot make content
trusted.

24
00:01:19.984 --> 00:01:21.251
Use a repeatable model.

25
00:01:21.331 --> 00:01:24.497
Identify the trust of the data and the
exact output context.

26
00:01:24.577 --> 00:01:27.554
Prefer an API that keeps data separate
from source code.

27
00:01:27.634 --> 00:01:32.257
When source generation is unavoidable,
encode for that specific parser and location.

28
00:01:32.337 --> 00:01:35.694
Sanitize only when the product
intentionally permits markup,

29
00:01:35.774 --> 00:01:39.194
then preserve the sanitizer's result
without unsafe mutation.

30
00:01:39.274 --> 00:01:41.680
Add browser controls as independent
layers.

31
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.370
XSS prevention is not a single escape
function applied everywhere.

32
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:49.060
It is a set of explicit decisions at every
interpretation boundary.
